{"causes":["The tribes of present-day Cambodia and Vietnam were unified under Jayavarman Kaundinya's rule.","He established a Hindu-Buddhist state in Funan, marking the beginning of the Funan era.","The Funan Kingdom was influenced by Indian culture and trade with India increased during his reign."],"effects":["The Funan Kingdom played a significant role in the development of Hinduism and Buddhism in Southeast Asia.","His son Senendup expanded the kingdom's borders, further solidifying its position in regional politics.","The statue of Jayavarman Kaundinya at Angkor Wat became a symbol of his enduring legacy."],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"senendup","sourceName":"Senendup","verb":"SUCCEEDS","targetSlug":"jayavarman-kaundinya","targetName":"Jayavarman Kaundinya","context":"His son Senendup succeeded him as the ruler of Funan."},{"sourceSlug":"angkor-wat","sourceName":"Angkor Wat","verb":"CONTAINS","targetSlug":"jayavarman-kaundinya-statue","targetName":"Jayavarman Kaundinya Statue","context":"The magnificent structure features intricate carvings that depict the Hindu gods and goddesses."},{"sourceSlug":"funan-era","sourceName":"Funan Era","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"jayavarman-kaundinya","targetName":"Jayavarman Kaundinya","context":"His reign marked the beginning of the Funan era, a significant period in Southeast Asian history."}],"places":[{"name":"Angkor Wat","role":"Birthplace and Legacy Site"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: Jayavarman Kaundinya","type":"Reference article","slug":"jayavarman-kaundinya"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q4160003","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jayavarman_Kaundinya"],"tags":["Monarchs & Rulers","political-systems","empire-and-colonialism","geopolitical-linkage"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"","summary":"Jayavarman (also identified as Jayavarman Kaundinya; died c. 514 CE) was an early king of the Funan kingdom — the first major organised polity in mainland Southeast Asia, centred in the lower Mekong delta (modern Cambodia and southern Vietnam) — and a figure of significant importance in the Indianisation of Southeast Asia. The name 'Kaundinya' connects him to the legendary founding tradition of Funan: according to Chinese chronicles and Cham inscriptions, the earliest rulers of Funan were associated with a legendary Indian Brahmin named Kaundinya who arrived by sea, married a local princess (sometimes called Soma or Willow-Leaf), and founded the royal dynasty — a founding myth that embodied the process of Indian cultural influence on the Southeast Asian coastal kingdoms.

The historical Jayavarman who died c. 514 CE was a late king of the Funan period whose reign is documented primarily through Chinese dynastic chronicles (the Liang Shu and Wei Shu) that record diplomatic missions between Funan and the Chinese courts of the Northern and Southern dynasties period. These missions — the exchange of diplomatic gifts, tribute, and occasionally monks between Funan and the Chinese courts — are among the primary documentary evidence for Funan's history, since the kingdom left few indigenous inscriptions. Jayavarman's reign falls in the period when Funan was beginning the transition that would eventually lead to its replacement by the Khmer Chenla kingdom (late 6th century CE).

Funan was a maritime trading power of major significance: positioned at the junction of the maritime trade routes connecting the Indian Ocean world with the South China Sea, its ports facilitated the exchange of Indian, Chinese, and Southeast Asian goods and the transmission of Indian cultural, religious, and administrative influences (Hinduism, Buddhism, Sanskrit literacy, Indian legal traditions) that fundamentally shaped the subsequent Khmer and other Southeast Asian civilisations. Jayavarman's reign was a late moment of this formative period of Southeast Asian Indianisation.","subjects":["Southeast Asian History","Funan Kingdom","Classical Era","Cambodia","Indianisation","Ancient History","Maritime Trade","Early Southeast Asia"],"frameworks":["CULTURAL_TRANSMISSION","WORLD_SYSTEMS","STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"Jayavarman Kaundinya's legacy extends beyond his own kingdom, as he laid the groundwork for future Southeast Asian civilizations.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
